# ── /join-us → /careers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The nav item has always read CAREERS and the URL said something else, which
# is a contradiction a reader can see in the status bar. The route moved; this
# keeps every link that was ever printed, bookmarked, emailed or indexed
# working.
#
# PERMANENT (308), not a temporary redirect: the old path is not coming back,
# and a 301/308 is what tells a crawler to transfer the page's history to the
# new URL rather than to keep both and pick one. The hash forms (#open-roles,
# #apply, and the per-job #ids in the page's JobPosting JSON-LD) survive for
# free — a fragment is never sent to the server, so the browser reapplies it
# to the redirect target.
#
# ── WHY HERE AND NOT IN next.config.mjs ──
# This lived in `redirects()` until the build moved to `output: "export"`.
# A redirect is something a server does, and an exported build has no server:
# Next prints a notice and silently drops the rule. Cloudflare Pages reads
# this file out of the published directory instead, and `public/` is copied
# into `out/` verbatim by the export — so this is the copy that is in force.
#
# Order matters: the first match wins, so the exact path is listed before the
# splat. `:splat` is Cloudflare's spelling of what Next wrote as `:path*`.
/join-us      /careers          308
/join-us/*    /careers/:splat   308

# ── /restaurants/<slug> → /restaurants ─────────────────────────────────────
# The per-venue detail pages are gone. Every surface that used to link one now
# points at the restaurant's own website instead, and there is no page here
# for Belly, Bintang, Ramo or any of the others.
#
# 308 TO THE INDEX RATHER THAN A 404, and the difference matters to the eight
# URLs Google has already indexed (they are sitelinks on the brand query). A
# 404 makes a reader who clicks one a dead end and makes the crawler drop the
# path with nothing to inherit it; a permanent redirect sends both to the page
# that now holds everything those pages held — the grid of all eight, each
# card carrying the menu and the venue's own site.
#
# THE SPLAT COVERS PATHS THAT NEVER EXISTED HERE, and one of them is why this
# is a splat: /restaurants/maginhawa is indexed and was never a route in this
# codebase, so it has been serving the 404 all along. It goes to the index
# with the rest rather than being listed by hand.
/restaurants/*    /restaurants    308
